            <ArticleTitle>Angiographically assessed coronary collateral circulation increases vulnerability to myocardial ischemia during vasodilator stress testing.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1419-24</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In patients with a major coronary vessel occluded, the presence of good coronary collateral circulation increases vulnerability to myocardial ischemia induced by pharmacologic coronary vasodilation. The presence of good collaterals results in a greater frequency, severity, and extent of dipyridamole-induced regional left ventricular dysfunction, consistent with the hypothesis of angiographically assessed collaterals facilitating &quot;steal phenomena&quot; during dipyridamole-induced coronary vasodilation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
